\title[Sharp curvature of the Lagrange exponent]
{Sharp Curvature of the Lagrange Exponent:\\ Concavity Above the Critical Mass $1/27$}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{Lagrange exponent} $\sigma:\R\to\R$, the global inverse of the critical cubic
$h(y)=y^{3}-y^{2}+\tfrac13 y$. This cubic is distinguished among monic cubics normalised by
$h(0)=0$ by the fact that its derivative is a perfect square, $h'(y)=3(y-\tfrac13)^{2}$: its two
critical points coalesce at $y=1/3$. Consequently $h$ is a strictly increasing bijection of $\R$,
Lagrange's resolvent degenerates to a single real radical, and
$\sigma(t)=\bigl(1+\cbrt{27t-1}\bigr)/3$ with the sign-aware real cube root. Our main theorem is
that $\sigma$ is strictly concave on $[1/27,\infty)$, so that averaging masses never decreases the
associated growth rate; we give the midpoint and Jensen forms. We prove the exact complementary
statement, strict convexity on $(-\infty,1/27]$, and deduce that the threshold is sharp: $\sigma$
is concave on $[c,\infty)$ if and only if $c\ge 1/27$, and convex on $(-\infty,c]$ if and only if
$c\le 1/27$. The inflection at $t=1/27$ is the image under $h$ of the degenerate critical point.
We then derive the cube-root sandwich $\cbrt{t}\le\sigma(t)\le\cbrt{t}+\tfrac13$ on the physical
range with both constants optimal; a merging law
$\sigma(s+t-\tfrac1{27})+\tfrac13\le\sigma(s)+\sigma(t)$ and its $n$-fold iterate, in which the
critical overhead is paid exactly once; the derivative $\sigma'(t)=3(27t-1)^{-2/3}$ on
$(1/27,\infty)$ with antitone derivative; and the order-isomorphism structure of $\sigma$. Finally
we exhibit the arithmetic origin of the constant: by the three-variable AM--GM inequality any
probability vector $(p,q,r)$ has $pqr\le 1/27$ with equality only at the uniform vector, whence
$\sigma(pqr)\le 1/3$ with equality iff $p=q=r=1/3$.
\end{abstract}

